From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: run a job's tasks in one HW kick (RK3576 submit
 floor)

rocket submits one NPU task per HW kick: rocket_job_hw_submit() programs a
single task's regcmd into PC_BASE_ADDRESS, sets PC_TASK_CON.TASK_NUMBER to
1, and the completion path re-arms the next task (next_task_idx++).  On
RK3576 that completion is a poll, and the poll IS the per-submit floor --
439 us with 0012's 50 us period.  A convolution that a row window splits
into n tasks therefore pays 439 us n times for work the program counter
could issue back to back, and every cost table on this part is a
submit-count table.

The PC can instead stream a contiguous run of self-chaining regcmds from a
single kick: program only the first task, set TASK_NUMBER = n, and the PC
executes one OP_ENABLE per task, following each task's embedded
PC_BASE_ADDRESS link to the next.  TASK_NUMBER gates a single completion.

Chaining is PER JOB, selected by userspace via a new uAPI flag, not a
global module param.  A job runs chained only when it carries
DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED -- set by userspace exactly for the jobs whose
regcmds it laid out contiguously and self-chained; any other job keeps the
stock per-task path.  So a chained-layout job and a gapped-layout one can
coexist in one process, which a global switch cannot express.

uAPI (include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h):
  - drm_rocket_job gains a trailing __u32 flags (+ __u32 reserved pad) and
    the DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED bit.  Appending it is safe because 0015
    made the submit descriptors extensible: the kernel guards on the v1
    minimum and copies with copy_struct_from_user(), so a userspace that
    predates @flags is accepted unchanged (flags zero-filled => stock
    per-task) and one that sets a flag this kernel does not know is refused
    with -E2BIG rather than silently downgraded.  Unknown flag bits are
    rejected with -EINVAL; @reserved must be zero.
  - The driver advertises interface version 1.1.  This is the capability
    check userspace needs: an older kernel does not know the flag and would
    run a self-chained layout down the per-task path, which stalls.
    Userspace MUST NOT self-chain unless DRM_IOCTL_VERSION reports >= 1.1.

kernel:
  - rocket_batch_submit (0644) is a MASTER switch over the per-job flag: 1
    (default) honors it, 0 forces every job to the stock path (a kill
    switch for bisecting the lever).  Userspace reads it at
    /sys/module/rocket/parameters/rocket_batch_submit and must not
    self-chain when it reads 0.
  - rocket_ioctl_submit_job captures the flag into rjob->want_batched.
  - rocket_job_run() decides job->batched once, before the first hw_submit:
    want_batched && master && task_count > 1 && the count fits this SoC's
    TASK_NUMBER width.  That width is per-SoC (0008): 12 bits on RK3588, 16
    on RK3576, so the bound is taken from rocket_soc_data rather than from
    the RK3588 field mask.
  - rocket_job_hw_submit() programs task 0 as before, sets TASK_NUMBER =
    task_count for a batched job, leaves TASK_DMA_BASE_ADDR = 0, and
    advances next_task_idx straight to task_count -- so the existing retire
    path fires the job's fence on the single completion instead of
    re-arming.  The completion path is otherwise unchanged.

This is the KERNEL HALF.  It requires userspace to lay a flagged job's
per-task regcmds out contiguously and rewrite each trailer to link to the
next (rocket-userspace, src/rocket_chain.c; ROCKET_RK3576_BATCH_TASKS for
the RK3576 convolution path).  A flagged job whose regcmds are NOT
contiguous runs task 0 and times out, recoverable.

This is the RK3576 analogue of the rk3588 series' patch 086.  It differs
in taking the TASK_NUMBER bound from the SoC data, and in that the RK3576
retires on a poll rather than an interrupt -- the single-completion
property is the same either way.

Measured on an H96 MAX M9 (RK3576).  Before, a concatenated multi-program
stream in one drm task ran task 0 and stopped, deterministically, and
27 shapes of the convolution library gate failed that way.  After, with
the chained layout and the flag, that gate is 156 passed / 7 refused as
required / 0 failed -- unchanged from the one-submit-per-task path, which
is the correctness bar.  The saving is one completion poll per row task
removed: a 32x32 k3 plane forced into 8 row windows goes 2.8 ms -> 1.0 ms,
and a 224x224 k3 s1 convolution 21.3 ms -> 19.1 ms.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c |  9 +++-
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 89 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h | 15 ++++++
 include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h   | 30 +++++++++++
 4 files changed, 134 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
index c019d0a30..a96944e91 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
@@ -154,7 +154,7 @@ static const struct drm_ioctl_desc rocket_drm_driver_ioctls[] = {
 DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
 
 /*
- * Rocket driver version.  Stock leaves .major/.minor unset, so a driver that has
+ * Rocket driver version. Stock leaves .major/.minor unset, so a driver that has
  * not been told to advertise anything reports 0.0.0; userspace reads this to
  * decide what the kernel guarantees.
  *
@@ -162,10 +162,15 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  *         and drm_rocket_task may be extended, the kernel accepts any size from
  *         the v1 layout upward, and a trailing field this kernel does not know
  *         is refused with -E2BIG rather than silently ignored.
+ * - 1.1 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED (per-job chained submit).
+ *
+ * Userspace MUST NOT self-chain a job's regcmd layout unless the driver reports
+ * >= 1.1: an older kernel does not know the flag, would run that chained layout
+ * down the stock per-task path, and the job corrupts or stalls.
  */
 static const struct drm_driver rocket_drm_driver = {
 	.major			= 1,
-	.minor			= 0,
+	.minor			= 1,
 	.driver_features	= DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL | DRIVER_GEM,
 	.open			= rocket_open,
 	.postclose		= rocket_postclose,
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 1d30507d8..385a8756c 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/iommu.h>
 #include <linux/kref.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/stddef.h>
@@ -69,6 +70,12 @@ static u32 rocket_pc_task_con(struct rocket_core *core, u32 task_number)
 	return (PC_TASK_CON_CTRL << core->soc->task_number_bits) | task_number;
 }
 
+/* The largest task count TASK_NUMBER can express on this SoC. */
+static u32 rocket_task_number_max(struct rocket_core *core)
+{
+	return (1u << core->soc->task_number_bits) - 1u;
+}
+
 /*
  * The submit ioctl's descriptors (drm_rocket_job, drm_rocket_task) are
  * EXTENSIBLE: userspace declares the size of the struct it was built against
@@ -87,12 +94,31 @@ static u32 rocket_pc_task_con(struct rocket_core *core, u32 task_number)
  * copy_struct_from_user() is the kernel's standard answer to both: it copies
  * min(usize, ksize), zero-fills the fields the caller did not supply, and
  * returns -E2BIG if userspace set anything in a trailing field this kernel does
- * not know about.  Growing either struct therefore needs no change here -- add
- * the field, keep the v1 offsetofend() guard, and both directions keep working.
+ * not know about.
  */
 #define ROCKET_JOB_V1_SIZE   offsetofend(struct drm_rocket_job, out_bo_handle_count)
 #define ROCKET_TASK_V1_SIZE  offsetofend(struct drm_rocket_task, regcmd_count)
 
+/*
+ * Batched (chained) submit.  A job runs batched only when userspace sets the
+ * per-job DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED flag (its regcmds are then laid out
+ * contiguously and self-chained): the kernel programs only task 0, sets
+ * PC_TASK_CON.TASK_NUMBER to the task count, and retires the whole job on the
+ * one completion that TASK_NUMBER gates.  A job without the flag keeps the
+ * stock one-submit-per-task path, so chained-layout and gapped-layout jobs
+ * coexist in one process.
+ *
+ * This module param is a master switch OVER the per-job flag: 1 (default)
+ * honors the flag, 0 forces every job to the stock per-task path (a global kill
+ * switch for bisecting the lever).  It is NOT the chaining decision itself.
+ * Userspace can read it at /sys/module/rocket/parameters/rocket_batch_submit
+ * and must not self-chain when it reads 0.
+ */
+static int rocket_batch_submit = 1;
+module_param(rocket_batch_submit, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rocket_batch_submit,
+		 "Master enable for per-job chained submit (DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED): 1 (default) honors the per-job flag, 0 forces every job to the stock per-task path.");
+
 static struct rocket_job *
 to_rocket_job(struct drm_sched_job *sched_job)
 {
@@ -191,7 +217,16 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 	/* GO ! */
 
 	task = &job->tasks[job->next_task_idx];
-	job->next_task_idx++;
+	/*
+	 * A batched job streams all of its tasks from this single kick, so jump
+	 * next_task_idx straight to the end: the one completion then retires the
+	 * whole job (the per-task re-arm in rocket_job_handle_irq is skipped).
+	 * The stock path advances one task at a time and is re-armed per IRQ.
+	 */
+	if (job->batched)
+		job->next_task_idx = job->task_count;
+	else
+		job->next_task_idx++;
 
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, BASE_ADDRESS, 0x1);
 
@@ -230,7 +265,9 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 				 PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_1);
 	}
 
-	rocket_pc_writel(core, TASK_CON, rocket_pc_task_con(core, 1));
+	rocket_pc_writel(core, TASK_CON,
+			 rocket_pc_task_con(core,
+					    job->batched ? job->task_count : 1));
 
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, TASK_DMA_BASE_ADDR, PC_TASK_DMA_BASE_ADDR_DMA_BASE_ADDR(0x0));
 
@@ -458,6 +495,18 @@ static struct dma_fence *rocket_job_run(struct drm_sched_job *sched_job)
 		core->attached_domain = job->domain;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Decide chained vs per-task once, here, before the first (and for a
+	 * batched job, only) hw_submit.  Gate on userspace having requested it
+	 * for THIS job (DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED -> want_batched), on the master
+	 * param, on there being more than one task to chain, and on the count
+	 * fitting this SoC's TASK_NUMBER width; anything else runs the stock
+	 * per-task path.
+	 */
+	job->batched = job->want_batched && rocket_batch_submit &&
+		       job->task_count > 1 &&
+		       job->task_count <= rocket_task_number_max(core);
+
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		core->in_flight_job = job;
 		rocket_job_hw_submit(core, job);
@@ -554,6 +603,13 @@ static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core)
 
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock)
 		if (core->in_flight_job) {
+			/*
+			 * A batched job advanced next_task_idx straight to
+			 * task_count in rocket_job_hw_submit, so it falls through
+			 * and retires on its single TASK_NUMBER-gated completion.
+			 * The stock path re-arms the next task here on each
+			 * per-task completion.
+			 */
 			if (core->in_flight_job->next_task_idx < core->in_flight_job->task_count) {
 				rocket_job_hw_submit(core, core->in_flight_job);
 				return;
@@ -804,6 +860,19 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 	if (job->task_count == 0)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/* Reject unknown flag bits so a future flag cannot be silently ignored. */
+	if (job->flags & ~DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED) {
+		drm_dbg(dev, "Unknown bits set in drm_rocket_job.flags (0x%x).\n",
+			job->flags);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	/* Keep @reserved claimable by a future field: it must arrive zeroed. */
+	if (job->reserved != 0) {
+		drm_dbg(dev, "Reserved field in drm_rocket_job struct should be 0.\n");
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
 	rjob = kzalloc_obj(*rjob);
 	if (!rjob)
 		return -ENOMEM;
@@ -812,6 +881,13 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 
 	rjob->rdev = rdev;
 
+	/*
+	 * Userspace's per-job chained-submit request.  rocket_job_run ANDs this
+	 * with the master param and the task-count constraints to decide
+	 * job->batched; a stock (gapped) job leaves the flag clear.
+	 */
+	rjob->want_batched = !!(job->flags & DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED);
+
 	/*
 	 * Take the IOMMU domain reference here, while the job is still empty.
 	 * rocket_job_cleanup() puts it unconditionally, and every rejection
@@ -901,9 +977,8 @@ int rocket_ioctl_submit(struct drm_device *dev, void *data, struct drm_file *fil
 	 * Report a rejected job.  The return was previously discarded, so SUBMIT
 	 * answered 0 even when a job failed validation -- userspace could not
 	 * tell a queued job from a dropped one, and every per-job check (task
-	 * sizes, regcmd_count, and any future flag validation) was unobservable.
-	 * Stop at the first failure and report it; jobs already queued keep
-	 * running.
+	 * sizes, regcmd_count, the flag validation above) was unobservable.  Stop
+	 * at the first failure and report it; jobs already queued keep running.
 	 */
 	for (i = 0; i < args->job_count; i++) {
 		ret = rocket_ioctl_submit_job(dev, file, &jobs[i]);
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
index 4ae00feec..02d3be8e5 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
@@ -30,6 +30,21 @@ struct rocket_job {
 	u32 task_count;
 	u32 next_task_idx;
 
+	/*
+	 * want_batched: userspace set DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED on this job (its
+	 * regcmds are then laid out contiguously and self-chained). Captured at
+	 * submit from drm_rocket_job.flags.
+	 *
+	 * batched: the effective decision, made once per job in rocket_job_run as
+	 * want_batched && the rocket_batch_submit master param && task_count > 1 &&
+	 * the count fits this SoC's TASK_NUMBER width. When set, the job's
+	 * self-chaining regcmds run from one HW kick (TASK_NUMBER = task_count, a
+	 * single completion) instead of one submit per task; read by the submit
+	 * and completion paths.
+	 */
+	bool want_batched;
+	bool batched;
+
 	/* Fence to be signaled by drm-sched once its done with the job */
 	struct dma_fence *inference_done_fence;
 
diff --git a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
index d0685e372..cc7898265 100644
--- a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
+++ b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
@@ -21,6 +21,22 @@ extern "C" {
 #define DRM_IOCTL_ROCKET_PREP_BO		DRM_IOW(DRM_COMMAND_BASE + DRM_ROCKET_PREP_BO, struct drm_rocket_prep_bo)
 #define DRM_IOCTL_ROCKET_FINI_BO		DRM_IOW(DRM_COMMAND_BASE + DRM_ROCKET_FINI_BO, struct drm_rocket_fini_bo)
 
+/*
+ * Flags for drm_rocket_job.flags.
+ *
+ * DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED: run this job's tasks as a single chained HW kick
+ * (PC_TASK_CON.TASK_NUMBER = task_count, one completion) instead of one submit
+ * + completion per task. The job's per-task regcmds must be laid out
+ * contiguously and self-chained by userspace; against a stock gapped layout the
+ * PC runs task 0 and the job times out. Per-job (not a module param) so a
+ * chained-layout job and a gapped-layout job can coexist in one process.
+ *
+ * Only honored by a driver advertising interface version >= 1.1 (DRM_IOCTL_VERSION).
+ * An older driver does not know the flag and would run a self-chained layout down
+ * the per-task path, so userspace must check the version before self-chaining.
+ */
+#define DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED		(1 << 0)
+
 /**
  * struct drm_rocket_create_bo - ioctl argument for creating Rocket BOs.
  *
@@ -155,6 +171,20 @@ struct drm_rocket_job {
 	 * (size is that times 4).
 	 */
 	__u32 out_bo_handle_count;
+
+	/**
+	 * @flags: Input: bitmask of DRM_ROCKET_JOB_* flags. Appended past the
+	 * original struct: a kernel that predates the field never reads it, and a
+	 * userspace that predates it declares a smaller @job_struct_size and has
+	 * the field zero-filled. Must be zero except for known flag bits.
+	 */
+	__u32 flags;
+
+	/**
+	 * @reserved: Reserved, must be zero. Pads @flags out to an 8-byte
+	 * boundary and leaves room for growth.
+	 */
+	__u32 reserved;
 };
 
